  user_input {
    user_response: "# Google AI Studio Prompt: Holy Bible \342\200\223 KJV 1611 Android App\n\nAct as a senior Android developer, UI/UX designer, and Bible application specialist. Create a complete, functional, lightweight native Android application named **Holy Bible \342\200\223 KJV 1611**.\n\nThe app should provide a simple and elegant way to read the King James Version of the Holy Bible, published in 1611, with Old Testament and New Testament browsing and a fast Bible verse search feature.\n\nThe app must be suitable for publishing on the Google Play Store.\n\n## 1. App Overview\n\n- **App name:** Holy Bible\n- **Bible version:** King James Version (KJV), 1611 edition\n- **Platform:** Native Android\n- **Language:** English\n- **Primary purpose:** Reading and searching Bible verses\n- **Internet requirement:** Core reading and search features must work completely offline.\n- **Account requirement:** No registration or login required.\n- **Design style:** Modern Christian aesthetic, peaceful, elegant, readable, and minimal.\n- **Performance:** Lightweight, fast startup, low memory usage, and optimized for budget Android phones.\n\n## 2. Christian Splash Screen\n\nCreate a beautiful, full-screen Christian splash screen that appears when the app launches.\n\nVisual design:\n- Deep navy blue or dark royal blue background.\n- A beautiful glowing golden cross positioned at the center.\n- Soft golden rays of light illuminating the cross.\n- Subtle clouds or a peaceful heavenly light effect in the background.\n- Elegant gold and white typography.\n- App title: \"HOLY BIBLE\"\n- Subtitle: \"KING JAMES VERSION \342\200\242 1611\"\n- Optional Bible-inspired tagline: \"Thy word is a lamp unto my feet, and a light unto my path.\" \342\200\224 Psalm 119:105\n\nKeep the splash screen reverent, peaceful, and visually impressive without excessive animations.\n\nUse a short, subtle fade-in animation. Automatically navigate to the home screen after initialization. Do not delay startup unnecessarily. Follow Android\'s recommended splash screen implementation and provide a separate designed welcome screen only if needed.\n\n## 3. Home Screen\n\nCreate a clean, modern home screen with the following layout:\n\n**Header**\n- A small cross icon.\n- App title: Holy Bible.\n- Subtitle: King James Version.\n- A search icon or prominent Bible search field.\n\n**Main content**\n- A prominent \"Continue Reading\" section if a previous reading position exists.\n- Two large, attractive buttons or cards:\n  - OLD TESTAMENT\n  - NEW TESTAMENT\n- Each card should have a simple Christian-themed icon, elegant colors, and rounded corners.\n- A \"Search Bible Verses\" section with a search input.\n- A \"Verse of the Day\" section displaying a Bible verse and its reference.\n\nUse a calm, elegant palette of navy blue, royal blue, warm white, and gold. Ensure text is readable and buttons are easy to tap.\n\nAvoid clutter, advertisements on the reading screen, excessive gradients, and unnecessary dashboard elements.\n\n## 4. Browse the Bible\n\nImplement complete browsing of all 66 books of the Bible.\n\n**Old Testament \342\200\224 39 books**\nGenesis, Exodus, Leviticus, Numbers, Deuteronomy, Joshua, Judges, Ruth, 1 Samuel, 2 Samuel, 1 Kings, 2 Kings, 1 Chronicles, 2 Chronicles, Ezra, Nehemiah, Esther, Job, Psalms, Proverbs, Ecclesiastes, Song of Solomon, Isaiah, Jeremiah, Lamentations, Ezekiel, Daniel, Hosea, Joel, Amos, Obadiah, Jonah, Micah, Nahum, Habakkuk, Zephaniah, Haggai, Zechariah, Malachi.\n\n**New Testament \342\200\224 27 books**\nMatthew, Mark, Luke, John, Acts, Romans, 1 Corinthians, 2 Corinthians, Galatians, Ephesians, Philippians, Colossians, 1 Thessalonians, 2 Thessalonians, 1 Timothy, 2 Timothy, Titus, Philemon, Hebrews, James, 1 Peter, 2 Peter, 1 John, 2 John, 3 John, Jude, Revelation.\n\nFeatures:\n- Display books grouped under Old Testament and New Testament.\n- Display the number of chapters for each book.\n- Tapping a book opens its chapter selection screen.\n- Tapping a chapter opens the complete chapter with numbered verses.\n- Provide previous chapter and next chapter navigation.\n- Include a clear back-navigation path.\n- Use smooth scrolling and preserve the user\'s reading position.\n- Make long chapters easy to read on mobile screens.\n\n## 5. Bible Reading Screen\n\nDesign a comfortable, distraction-free reading experience.\n\nRequirements:\n- Display the book name and chapter number at the top.\n- Display the complete chapter text with numbered verses.\n- Use a readable serif or highly legible font.\n- Use appropriate line spacing and paragraph spacing.\n- Allow users to adjust text size.\n- Include previous and next chapter buttons.\n- Allow users to tap a verse to reveal actions:\n  - Copy verse\n  - Share verse\n  - Bookmark verse\n- Display references in the correct format, such as John 3:16.\n- Provide a clear visual distinction between verse numbers and Bible text.\n- Include a bookmark list accessible from the home screen or navigation menu.\n- Save the last-read book and chapter locally so users can continue reading.\n\nDo not insert invented verses or substitute paraphrases for the actual KJV text.\n\n## 6. Bible Verse Search\n\nImplement a fully functional offline search engine.\n\nSearch features:\n- Search by keywords or phrases within the Bible text.\n- Support partial-word searches where appropriate.\n- Display matching verses with their book, chapter, and verse references.\n- Highlight the matching search terms.\n- Allow users to tap a result to open the correct chapter and scroll to the selected verse.\n- Provide a clear search button and a way to clear the query.\n- Display a friendly message when no matches are found.\n- Support searches such as:\n  - faith\n  - love\n  - forgiveness\n  - Jesus wept\n  - John 3:16\n  - Psalm 23\n- When a user enters a reference such as John 3:16, recognize it as a Bible reference and open the corresponding verse.\n- Keep search fast and responsive, even on lower-end devices.\n\nSearch must work without an internet connection.\n\n## 7. Verse of the Day\n\nAdd a Verse of the Day card to the home screen.\n\nRequirements:\n- Display an actual verse from the included KJV Bible dataset.\n- Show the full Bible reference.\n- Use a deterministic daily selection so the same verse appears throughout the day.\n- Allow users to copy, share, bookmark, or open the verse.\n- Do not generate Bible verses using AI.\n- Do not display placeholder text after the app has been configured.\n\n## 8. Bookmarks and Sharing\n\nImplement local bookmarks without requiring user registration.\n\nUsers must be able to:\n- Bookmark and unbookmark individual verses.\n- View all bookmarked verses.\n- Tap a bookmark to open the relevant chapter.\n- Remove bookmarks.\n- Copy a verse with its reference.\n- Share a verse through Android\'s native sharing menu.\n\nExample copied text:\n\n\"For God so loved the world, that he gave his only begotten Son, that whosoever believeth in him should not perish, but have everlasting life. \342\200\224 John 3:16 (KJV)\"\n\nUse the actual verse text from the local Bible dataset.\n\n## 9. Offline Bible Database\n\nThis is one of the most important requirements.\n\nBundle the complete Bible text in the application so that reading, browsing, and searching work without internet access.\n\nRequirements:\n- Include all 66 books, all chapters, and every verse.\n- Use the actual KJV text, not generated or fabricated content.\n- Store the data efficiently using a local SQLite database or another appropriate local database solution.\n- Consider Room for structured local storage and SQLite full-text search (FTS) for fast searching.\n- Include stable book IDs, testament classification, chapter numbers, verse numbers, and the actual verse text.\n- Load the initial data locally on first launch without requiring a network download.\n- Ensure the database is packaged with the app or reliably initialized from bundled assets.\n- Do not rely on an external API or online Bible website for essential functionality.\n- Do not leave TODOs or placeholder Bible content.\n\n**Important:** The 1611 historical edition and the modernized KJV text are not necessarily identical in spelling, typography, and editorial details. Identify the precise source edition used. If the exact 1611 historical text is unavailable, do not falsely label a modernized KJV text as a verified original 1611 transcription. Explain the source clearly and keep the version label accurate.\n\nUse a verified, legally distributable Bible dataset. Include attribution or source documentation if required. Confirm the licensing status of the selected source and any bundled database assets before release.\n\n## 10. Navigation\n\nProvide simple navigation between:\n- Home\n- Old Testament\n- New Testament\n- Bible Search\n- Bookmarks\n- Continue Reading\n\nUse a compact bottom navigation bar or another intuitive navigation design. Keep the number of navigation items manageable and avoid duplicating functions.\n\nEnsure Android system back navigation works correctly on all screens.\n\n## 11. Technical Requirements\n\nUse the following technologies where compatible with the current Google AI Studio Android project workflow:\n\n- Kotlin\n- Jetpack Compose\n- Material 3\n- Android Navigation Compose\n- Room or SQLite for local Bible data and bookmarks\n- Kotlin coroutines and Flow where appropriate\n- Android Sharesheet for sharing verses\n- DataStore or an equivalent lightweight local persistence mechanism for preferences and reading position\n\nAdditional requirements:\n- Use a clean, maintainable architecture.\n- Separate Bible data, search logic, navigation, and UI components.\n- Follow modern Android development practices.\n- Support current Android SDK requirements for Google Play submission.\n- Configure the app name, launcher icon, theme, and package name correctly.\n- Provide appropriate adaptive launcher icons.\n- Handle empty states, database initialization, and errors gracefully.\n- Request no unnecessary permissions.\n- Do not require Firebase, an account, or a backend for core functionality.\n- Do not include tracking or analytics unless explicitly requested.\n- Do not add ads, subscriptions, or in-app purchases.\n\n## 12. Visual Design Requirements\n\nMake the app look like a polished Christian Bible-reading application rather than a generic database viewer.\n\nUse:\n- Deep navy blue for the main brand color.\n- Gold for the cross, selected states, and small highlights.\n- Warm white or light cream for reading backgrounds.\n- Dark, highly readable text.\n- Rounded cards with subtle elevation.\n- Clean typography and consistent spacing.\n- Tasteful Christian icons.\n- Smooth but restrained transitions.\n- Proper light and dark theme support if practical.\n\nKeep the Bible text as the primary focus. Do not sacrifice readability for decoration.\n\n## 13. Final Validation\n\nBefore considering the app complete, verify all of the following:\n\n1. The application builds successfully.\n2. The splash screen displays correctly.\n3. All 66 books are available.\n4. Every book opens the correct chapters.\n5. Every chapter displays its complete verse text.\n6. Bible search returns actual matching verses.\n7. Bible reference searches open the correct locations.\n8. Copy and share functions work.\n9. Bookmarks persist after closing and reopening the app.\n10. Continue Reading restores the correct reading position.\n11. Verse of the Day displays real KJV text.\n12. The core app works in airplane mode.\n13. No screen contains fabricated Bible text, dead buttons, or placeholder functionality.\n14. Text remains readable on small screens.\n15. The app launches without crashes or missing database assets.\n\n## 14. Deliverables\n\nGenerate the complete Android application, not merely a UI mockup or a design concept.\n\nProvide:\n- All necessary Kotlin source files.\n- All Compose UI screens and reusable components.\n- Navigation implementation.\n- The complete, verified, legally distributable Bible dataset and database initialization logic.\n- Search, bookmarking, copy, share, and reading-position functionality.\n- Splash screen and launcher icon resources.\n- Required Gradle dependencies and configuration.\n- Instructions for opening, building, and running the app in Android Studio.\n- A final checklist of anything that still requires manual setup.\n\n**Do not stop after creating the home screen.** Implement the working application end to end. If a large Bible dataset cannot be generated or bundled in one response, state that limitation explicitly and provide a reliable, reproducible way to integrate a verified complete dataset. Never silently replace missing Bible text with sample verses.\n\nPrioritize accuracy, offline functionality, ease of use, visual polish, and a successful Android build.\n[AIS_METADATA_SECTION_START]\nInstructions: If you need more context from the user you must first ask the user some questions using the ask_question tool. If you ask questions, you must wait for the user\'s answers. After the questions you must present a plan.\n[AIS_METADATA_SECTION_END]"
